  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale:\nThe solution employs a hierarchical optimization strategy that combines global search with local refinement. Unlike previous methods that are grid-centered, this approach leverages function-space optimization by transforming the step-function representation into a smooth optimization problem. Key improvements include: (a) using a gradient-based estimator with dynamic bounding, (b) multi-scale optimization with exponential decay of the gradient strength, and (c) analytical gradient estimation for the maximum overlap criterion. This method avoids the combinatorial bottlenecks of grid-constrained optimization while maintaining the cross-discontinuity properties required.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification:\nThe optimization min-max formulation is translated into:  \nminimize \u2225\u2207C(h)\u2225\u2082 subject to \u222bh(x)dx=1, 0\u2264h(x)\u22641  \nwhere C(h) = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx  \nThe gradient is approximated via:  \n\u2207_i h_j = \u222bk[x_j]h_k(1-h(x+k))_k dx  \nwhere k[x_j] is a geometric kernel weighting (decay factor \u03b1=0.9).  \n\n3. Implementation Procedure:\nDeploy a covariance-guided coordinate descent:\n\nPhase 1: Global initialization\n- Start with uniform grid n=700 if parent exists, otherwise sample random chains\n- Fit Gaussian process prior to smooth h(x)\n- Heuristic projection: minimize \u222b(h-h_mean)^2dx subject to integral=0.5\n\nPhase 2: Contrained optimization\n- Initialize h(x) from the parent solution via exponential smoothing\n- Compute functional value: C_est = \u222bcorr(h,1-h)(x)dx\n- Dynamic gradient computation:\n  For each point i:\n  h_i = clip(h_i - \u03b1\u00b7grad_est, 0, 1)\n  where \u03b1 adaptively scales by (1-|\u2202C/\u2202h_i|)\n\nPhase 3: Iterative refinement\n- Local search directions: \n  d_i = (\u2202C/\u2202h_i - \u03b2\u00b7min_j\u2207C_j)\u00b7(1-h_i)\n- Implementation flow:\n  while time remaining and improvement threshold:\n    update gradients\n    projected gradients onto feasible set\n    adaptive learning rate decay\n    1-step lookahead for correlation minima\n   \n4. Search Choices:\n- Time allocation: 50% to gradient approximation, 30% to adaptive learning\n- Geometry approximation: use radial basis functions for cross-correlation\n- Dimension reduction: project high-frequency components via discrete sine transform\n- Random restarts: triggered by stagnation of improvement below 0.0001 for 3 consecutive steps\n\n5. Budget Planning:\n- Coefficient penalty: \u03b2=0.95 for gradient damping\n- Max grid size: 1600 points (linear to maintain within budget)\n- Problem dimensionality: use n_eff = n/(8*log(n)) for reduced space optimization\n- Parallel execution: 1 CPU core but 4 virtual processes for gradient approximation\n\n6. Validation:\nPost-process every candidate solution with:\n- compute_c5(h)\n- Double-check 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 by element-wise projection\n- Verify integral by: n/2 == sum(h)\n- Analysis of correlation structure: ensure max correlation decay",
